 mean differs from the others.\n\nIndependent random samples are conducted, with $10$ individuals drawn from the first population, $12$ from the second, and $18$ from the third. It is found that $SSB = 24.5$ and $SSW = 166.5$.\n\nThe table below gives the values of $x$ that satisfy $P(X > x) = 5%$, where $X∼F(ν_{1}, ν_{2})$.\n\n| $ν_{2}∖ν_{1}$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $37$ | $3.252$ | $2.859$ | $2.626$ |\n| $38$ | $3.245$ | $2.852$ | $2.619$ |\n| $39$ | $3.238$ | $2.845$ | $2.612$ |\n| $40$ | $3.232$ | $2.839$ | $2.606$ |\n\nUsing this information, select the options that make the following statements true.\n\nThe $F$-ratio for this test is approximately\n{{field-1}}.\n\nAt the $5%$ level of significance, there is\n{{field-2}}\nevidence that at least one mean differs from the others.\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204474 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3.771" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186202822} {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3.329" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186202823} {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.901" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186202824} {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.295" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186202825}] :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186202821} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "sufficient" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186202827} {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "insufficient" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186202828}] :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186202826}] :question/math-academy-id "q-252478" :question/problem "An IT company is evaluating customer satisfaction across four different laptop brands: Brand A, Brand B, Brand 